The paper rules out the naive bounded-clique-width reading as Track A: Theorem 3.18 gives an XP dynamic program, while Proposition 3.1 supplies the genuine hard regime only when module-width grows. Its reduction is unusually useful here: add a \(k\)-clique to a \(k\)-coloring instance, and every proper coloring becomes a native \(b\)-coloring whose clique vertices are the \(b\)-witnesses. I’m now checking whether a planted high-width coloring distribution survives the required specialist attacks; worst-case hardness alone will not be used as evidence.
