-This generator turns Jaffke, Lima, and Lokshtanov's [*b-Coloring
-Parameterized by Clique-Width*](https://arxiv.org/abs/2003.04254) into a
-search for a compact affine fall coloring. The solver receives a finite simple
-7-regular graph and a distinct binary coordinate on each vertex. It returns a
-full-rank `3 x d` matrix and a three-bit offset over `GF(2)`. The affine map
-must color the graph with eight colors so that every closed neighborhood
-contains all eight colors. This is exactly a fall coloring and hence a
-b-coloring in which every vertex is a b-vertex.
+## Problem and trust model
 
-The certificate is checked without a theorem oracle: `verify` expands the
-matrix on vertex coordinates, compares the colors on every edge, and compares
-every closed-neighborhood color set with `{0,...,7}`. It never reads the
-planted answer. The local correctness and adversary gates pass. The required
-four-vendor oracle call did **not** complete because the supplied OpenRouter key
-returned HTTP 403 `Key limit exceeded` on every redraw. Therefore the module is
-locally verified but is not ready to submit as hardened until those arms are
-rerun with available quota.
+This module instantiates the paper [*b-Coloring Parameterized by Clique-Width*](https://arxiv.org/abs/2003.04254), specifically the fall-coloring object in Section 3.6. A fall coloring is a proper coloring in which every vertex has a neighbor in every other color, so every vertex is a `b`-vertex. The solver receives a finite simple 7-regular graph, a distinct GF(2) coordinate for each vertex, and an affine coordinate frame. It must return a full-rank three-row binary matrix and a three-bit offset whose eight induced colors form a fall coloring. `verify` expands that map, checks every edge, and checks every closed neighborhood using exact binary arithmetic; it never reads the planted answer.
 
-## Why Track B
+Generation is inverse. The module samples the affine certificate first, draws equally many coordinates in each of its eight fibers, and connects every pair of fibers by an independently shuffled perfect matching. Consequently each closed neighborhood contains one vertex of each color. Connectivity and promised presentation properties are checked during rejection sampling, but the answer is never obtained by solving the generated graph.
 
-Section 2.2 gives the exact b-coloring definition. Section 3.6 defines Fall
-Coloring, and Theorem 3.20 solves it in `n^(2^O(w))` time when a rooted branch
-decomposition of module-width `w` is supplied. Proposition 3.21 proves a
-matching parameterized lower bound, but only for worst-case instances. It does
-not make a planted random distribution hard, so this module makes no Track A
-claim. The easy cases that must not be hidden are Corollary 2.10
-(`2^O(vc^2) n` for vertex-cover number `vc`), Corollary 2.11
-(`2^O(k^2) n` on chordal graphs), and bounded clique-width itself.
+## Why Track B
 
-Generation samples the affine answer first. It draws equally many coordinates
-in each of its eight fibres, then joins every pair of fibres by an independent
-perfect matching. Each vertex consequently has one neighbor of every other
-color, which proves the planted answer is a fall coloring by construction.
+This is deliberately not a Track A claim. The paper's Theorem 3.20 gives an `n^(2^O(w))` dynamic program when a module-width-`w` branch decomposition is supplied; Proposition 3.21 gives matching worst-case parameterized lower bounds, but says nothing about this generated distribution. Corollary 2.10 is FPT in vertex-cover number, and Corollary 2.11 is FPT for chordal graphs parameterized by the number of colors. Those results rule out pretending that every structured instance is hard.
 
-An efficient algorithm exists and is disclosed. Exact Gaussian elimination
-over `GF(2)` finds the nullspace of adjacency-plus-identity, quotients its one
-nuisance direction, recovers the eight cover fibres, and fits an affine map.
-It runs in `O(n^3/word_size)` packed operations and solved 8/8 shipping
-instances. The final benchmark used 15,757,327 packed exact operations and
-15.169 seconds in total, at most 1,978,355 operations for one instance. The
-compact route notices that the XOR of the eight coordinates in any closed
-neighborhood lies in the hidden matrix kernel. By construction the first 15
-such signatures span the 13-dimensional kernel; exact elimination then needs
-at most 253 packed operations. That compression gap—not complexity-theoretic
-hardness—is the Track B claim.
+For this distribution, a construction-specific polynomial algorithm exists and is disclosed: recover the cover fibers from the nullspace of adjacency-plus-identity over GF(2), then fit an affine projection. Packed exact Gaussian elimination costs `O(n^3/word_size)`. On eight shipping instances it solved 8/8 in 15.169 seconds and 15,757,327 packed exact operations, at most 1,978,355 on one instance. The compact route notices that the XOR of the eight coordinates in a closed neighborhood is killed by the coloring matrix; a handful of these signatures exposes its three-dimensional kernel. The measured compact route uses at most 253 packed exact operations. The benchmark tests whether a no-tool solver finds that invariant, not whether the instances defeat software.
 
