   107	NOTES = r"""
   108	Paper grounding and Step 0.  Section 2.2 defines a b-coloring as a proper
   109	coloring in which every color class contains a vertex adjacent to all other
   110	colors.  Section 3.6 defines a fall coloring as the special case in which every
   111	vertex has that property, and Theorem 3.20 gives an n^(2^O(w)) dynamic program
   112	when a module-width-w branch decomposition is supplied.  Proposition 3.21
   113	shows matching parameterized lower bounds, but only in the worst case; it does
   114	not establish hardness for a planted distribution.  Consequently this module
   115	is explicitly Track B, never Track A.
   116	
   117	What produces the certificate.  A domain-standard exact route recovers the
   118	eight fibres of the K8 lift from the nullspace of adjacency-plus-identity over
   119	GF(2), then fits an affine projection.  It is polynomial and succeeds on this
   120	distribution, so it is reported separately as the Track B reference algorithm.
   121	The intended compact route uses the coordinate XOR of a few closed
   122	neighborhoods.  Their images are the XOR of all eight GF(2)^3 values, namely
   123	zero, so the signatures lie in the kernel of the coloring matrix.  Generation
   124	ensures the first d-1 signatures span the whole (d-3)-dimensional kernel.
   125	
   126	Generation.  The affine matrix and offset are sampled first.  Equal numbers of
   127	distinct coordinates are sampled in its eight fibres.  For each pair of
   128	fibres an independent random perfect matching is added.  Thus every vertex has
   129	one neighbor of each other color and is a b-vertex.  Matchings and presentation
   130	are resampled only to require connectivity, the advertised compact invariant,
   131	and clean reference-algorithm recovery; the held certificate never comes from
   132	solving a generated instance.
   133	
   134	Easy regimes and attacks.  The paper gives polynomial algorithms for bounded
   135	clique-width, vertex cover (Corollary 2.10), and chordal graphs parameterized by
   136	the number of colors (Corollary 2.11).  This generated distribution has its own
   137	polynomial spectral/nullspace decoder as well.  Cheap attacks try coordinate
   138	outliers, greedy no-backtracking fall coloring, random affine restarts, and the
   139	three-low-bits ansatz.  Random coordinate frames, dense hidden rows, and random
   140	matchings remove those signatures; exact reference recovery remains successful
   141	and is disclosed rather than misreported as a failure.
   142	""".strip()
