     1	# Affine fall-coloring generator for arXiv:2003.04254
     2	
     3	| profile field | value |
     4	|---|---|
     5	| track | **B — no-tool compression** |
     6	| native domain | combinatorics |
     7	| object regime | finite field |
     8	| computational core | graph |
     9	| certificate form | matrix certificate |
    10	| intuition | invariant: closed-neighborhood coordinate XORs lie in the hidden coloring map's kernel |
    11	| domain essentiality | native |
    12	| reduction | none |
    13	
    14	## Problem and trust model
    15	
    16	This module instantiates the paper [*b-Coloring Parameterized by Clique-Width*](https://arxiv.org/abs/2003.04254), specifically the fall-coloring object in Section 3.6. A fall coloring is a proper coloring in which every vertex has a neighbor in every other color, so every vertex is a `b`-vertex. The solver receives a finite simple 7-regular graph, a distinct GF(2) coordinate for each vertex, and an affine coordinate frame. It must return a full-rank three-row binary matrix and a three-bit offset whose eight induced colors form a fall coloring. `verify` expands that map, checks every edge, and checks every closed neighborhood using exact binary arithmetic; it never reads the planted answer.
    17	
    18	Generation is inverse. The module samples the affine certificate first, draws equally many coordinates in each of its eight fibers, and connects every pair of fibers by an independently shuffled perfect matching. Consequently each closed neighborhood contains one vertex of each color. Connectivity and promised presentation properties are checked during rejection sampling, but the answer is never obtained by solving the generated graph.
    19	
    20	## Why Track B
    21	
    22	This is deliberately not a Track A claim. The paper's Theorem 3.20 gives an `n^(2^O(w))` dynamic program when a module-width-`w` branch decomposition is supplied; Proposition 3.21 gives matching worst-case parameterized lower bounds, but says nothing about this generated distribution. Corollary 2.10 is FPT in vertex-cover number, and Corollary 2.11 is FPT for chordal graphs parameterized by the number of colors. Those results rule out pretending that every structured instance is hard.
    23	
    24	For this distribution, a construction-specific polynomial algorithm exists and is disclosed: recover the cover fibers from the nullspace of adjacency-plus-identity over GF(2), then fit an affine projection. Packed exact Gaussian elimination costs `O(n^3/word_size)`. On eight shipping instances it solved 8/8 in 15.169 seconds and 15,757,327 packed exact operations, at most 1,978,355 on one instance. The compact route notices that the XOR of the eight coordinates in a closed neighborhood is killed by the coloring matrix; a handful of these signatures exposes its three-dimensional kernel. The measured compact route uses at most 253 packed exact operations. The benchmark tests whether a no-tool solver finds that invariant, not whether the instances defeat software.
    25	
    26	## Worked demo
    27	
    28	The `demo` preset with seed 3 is small enough to solve on paper by XORing a few five-bit closed neighborhoods and doing GF(2) elimination:
    29	
    30	```text
    31	Affine certificate for an eight-color fall coloring
    32	n = 16, d = 5; vertices are 0..15; all arithmetic is in GF(2).
    33	
    34	Frame origin: 01001
    35	Frame directions:
    36	0: 11011
    37	1: 10010
    38	2: 10000
    39	3: 01101
    40	4: 10101
    41	
