		But this implies that $v$ has a neighbor in $C_s \subseteq C_t$,
		a contradiction.
		
		Now suppose that $\cdesc_t(Q) = \cdemand$. 
		By the definition of the merge type, we have that in this case:
		%
		\begin{enumerate}
		    \item\label{claim:dem:1}
		    For any $p \in \{r, s\}$ and $Q_p \in V_p/{\sim_p}$ with $\bubblemap_p(Q_p) = Q$, $\cdesc_p(Q_p) \neq \ccontains$.
		    \item\label{claim:dem:2} 
		    We may assume (up to renaming) that for some $Q_r \in \bubblemap_r^{-1}(Q)$, $\cdesc_r(Q_r) = \cdemand$, 
		    \item\label{claim:dem:3}
