 ## Problem and trust model
 
-This module instantiates the paper [*b-Coloring Parameterized by Clique-Width*](https://arxiv.org/abs/2003.04254), specifically the fall-coloring object in Section 3.6. A fall coloring is a proper coloring in which every vertex has a neighbor in every other color, so every vertex is a `b`-vertex. The solver receives a finite simple 7-regular graph, a distinct GF(2) coordinate for each vertex, and an affine coordinate frame. It must return a full-rank three-row binary matrix and a three-bit offset whose eight induced colors form a fall coloring. `verify` expands that map, checks every edge, and checks every closed neighborhood using exact binary arithmetic; it never reads the planted answer.
+This module instantiates the fall-coloring problem treated in Section 3.6 of [*b-Coloring Parameterized by Clique-Width*](https://arxiv.org/abs/2003.04254). A fall coloring is a proper coloring in which every vertex has a neighbor in every other color, so every vertex is a `b`-vertex. The solver receives a finite simple 7-regular graph, distinct GF(2) coordinates on its vertices, and an affine coordinate frame. It must return a full-rank three-row binary matrix and three-bit offset whose eight induced colors form a fall coloring. Although the instance contains a graph, the searched certificate and intended route are exact GF(2) linear algebra. `verify` expands the affine map, checks every edge, and checks every closed neighborhood exactly; it never reads the planted answer.
 
-Generation is inverse. The module samples the affine certificate first, draws equally many coordinates in each of its eight fibers, and connects every pair of fibers by an independently shuffled perfect matching. Consequently each closed neighborhood contains one vertex of each color. Connectivity and promised presentation properties are checked during rejection sampling, but the answer is never obtained by solving the generated graph.
+Generation is inverse. The module samples the affine certificate first, draws equally many coordinates in its eight fibers, and connects every pair of fibers by an independently shuffled perfect matching. Every closed neighborhood therefore contains exactly one vertex of every color. Rejection sampling enforces connectivity and the promised presentation properties, but it never obtains the returned certificate by solving the generated instance.
 
 ## Why Track B
 
-This is deliberately not a Track A claim. The paper's Theorem 3.20 gives an `n^(2^O(w))` dynamic program when a module-width-`w` branch decomposition is supplied; Proposition 3.21 gives matching worst-case parameterized lower bounds, but says nothing about this generated distribution. Corollary 2.10 is FPT in vertex-cover number, and Corollary 2.11 is FPT for chordal graphs parameterized by the number of colors. Those results rule out pretending that every structured instance is hard.
+This is not a Track A claim. Theorem 3.20 gives an `n^(2^O(w))` fall-coloring algorithm when a module-width-`w` branch decomposition is supplied, and Proposition 3.21 gives a matching worst-case parameterized lower bound; neither proves hardness for this generated distribution. For `b`-coloring, Corollary 2.10 is FPT in vertex-cover number and Corollary 2.11 is FPT on chordal graphs parameterized by the number of colors. The paper also records polynomial fall-coloring cases for strongly chordal, threshold, and split graphs. The generator claims none of those regimes.
 
-For this distribution, a construction-specific polynomial algorithm exists and is disclosed: recover the cover fibers from the nullspace of adjacency-plus-identity over GF(2), then fit an affine projection. Packed exact Gaussian elimination costs `O(n^3/word_size)`. On eight shipping instances it solved 8/8 in 1.143 seconds and 15,757,327 packed exact operations, at most 1,978,355 on one instance. The compact route notices that the XOR of the eight coordinates in a closed neighborhood is killed by the coloring matrix; a handful of these signatures exposes its three-dimensional kernel. The measured compact route uses at most 253 packed exact operations. The benchmark tests whether a no-tool solver finds that invariant, not whether the instances defeat software.
+For this distribution, a construction-specific polynomial decoder exists and is disclosed: take the nullspace of adjacency-plus-identity over GF(2), recover the eight cover fibers, and fit an affine projection. Packed exact Gaussian elimination costs `O(n^3/word_size)`. On eight shipping instances it solved 8/8 in 0.222 seconds total and 1,023,502 packed exact operations, with at most 129,261 operations for one instance. That is easy for software but out of reach by hand. The compact route notices that each closed-neighborhood coordinate XOR lies in the kernel of the coloring map; exact elimination on a few such signatures used at most 262 packed operations. The benchmark tests whether a no-tool solver finds this compression, not whether the instances defeat software.
 
